@packages/sigilc/scope.sigil from SigilComparisonScope import { ScopeInput, DesignMembership }
@packages/sigilc/report.sigil from SigilGateDiagnostics import { ReportSchema, NativeFindings }
@packages/cli/_module.sigil from SigilCli import { DesignExport }
@packages/lsp/_module.sigil from SigilLsp import { LspPackage, NavigationAndInspection, DocumentRendering, OwnershipSourceWatching }

component SigilVsCodeExtension {
  goal {
    Provide a VS Code authoring and review surface for file-backed Sigil
    workspaces.

    Connect editor-native features to the bundled language server without
    duplicating deterministic Sigil semantics.
  }

  interface {
    ExtensionPackage {
      provides an installable VSIX whose artifact version is owned by package.json

      the VSIX is distributed through a versioned GitHub Release
    }

    EditorLanguageSupport {
      registers the sigil language for .sigil files

      provides TextMate syntax highlighting before extension-host activation

      starts the bundled language server through vscode-languageclient

      surfaces diagnostics, document symbols, definition navigation, hover, and
      semantic highlighting

      accepts the bundled server's dynamic registration for supported ownership
      implementation-source changes
    }

    DocumentPreview {
      contributes a Sigil: Open Preview command and an editor-title action for Sigil
      documents

      renders the whole active .sigil file to Markdown and opens it as a read-only
      preview beside the editor
    }

    SupportedExtensionHosts {
      runs as a workspace extension in trusted desktop and remote Node extension
      hosts

      supports file-backed workspaces and file URIs

      does not support VS Code for the Web or virtual workspaces
    }

    ReadOnlyEditorSupport {
      collects no telemetry

      does not modify Sigil documents
    }

    CompilationSurface {
      Use DesignExport for structural export, ScopeInput and DesignMembership for
      native file membership, and NativeFindings for compiler-owned presentation.
      Contribute Compile File and Compile Workspace with Design or Implementation
      focus. Export current structure through sigil export design, then invoke native
      sigilc directly and display its named state and native diagnostics. File focus
      uses the active physical Sigil file as an ordered native Design root; native
      scope adds imports and structural owners. Show the effective scope honestly,
      including wider dependencies, rather than claiming a cursor-selected component
      was independently compiled. Implementation focus requires an explicit native
      source-selection JSON path. No model invocation, compiler package, profile or
      legacy event adapter.
    }
  }

  state {
    EditorLanguageSupport {
      The language client lifecycle is stopped, starting, running, or stopping.
    }

    DocumentPreview {
      Preview reflects the active Sigil editor's whole document when the command
      runs, independent of cursor position.
    }

    CompilationSurface {
      The current operation is starting, running, completed, unavailable, failed,
      cancelled or stale. A replacement cancels and supersedes the previous run.
      Focus is ask, design or implementation; displayed semantic state comes directly
      from the completed native gate, never from diagnostic absence.
    }
  }

  logic {
    EditorLanguageSupport {
      Opening or activating a Sigil document starts the bundled server when it is not
      already running.

      The language client forwards document lifecycle and language-feature requests
      through LSP.

      The language client delivers file events for watcher patterns dynamically
      registered by the bundled server.

      Startup and runtime failures are written to the Sigil output channel and
      surfaced to the user.

      Deactivation stops the language client and server process.
    }

    DocumentPreview {
      The preview command requests a whole-document Markdown render from the language
      server for the active file.

      A successful render opens beside the source in the built-in Markdown preview.

      An empty render or an unavailable language server shows a non-destructive
      informational message.

      The editor-title action invokes the same preview command.
    }

    CompilationSurface {
      Export the complete file-backed workspace to a temporary frontend bundle.
      Design Workspace invokes compile design. Implementation Workspace invokes
      compile implementation with the configured native selection. File commands
      supply a native scope with that ordered Design root and the Implementation
      selection. Design-only file scope explicitly excludes all Implementation files;
      it makes no Implementation comparison claim. Native source selection and
      dependency closure remain compiler-owned. File compilation uses its containing
      workspace; multi-root workspace compilation uses the active folder or asks for
      an eligible folder. Keep stdout as one native JSON report and stderr as
      operational progress. Display findings at their native locations and retain
      bounded witnesses in output. Missing code ranges stay file-only. Report omitted
      finding counts. Use exactly Coherent/Loose/Disjoint and Closed/Converged/Drift
      with their native green/yellow/red mapping. Unavailable comparison is not a
      color. Refuse dirty workspace documents before capture. Clear displayed
      findings and mark stale after workspace document, disk or relevant settings
      changes. Cancel on replacement or extension deactivation; discard superseded
      results.
    }
  }

  constraints {

    CurrentLanguageAndLocations {
      TextMate grammar, bundled language services, preview, and native commands
      target the same 0.8 authority. Highlight explicit owner-file/component Tag
      selections, all seven contracts, local grouping and inline Tags, links, and
      inert payloads without legacy expand or directory-import fallback.

      Consume ReportSchema native report schema 2 independently of frontend schema 2.
      Convert every Sigil byte range, including related and destination locations, to
      UTF-16 using the exact captured source snapshot. If only disk text is
      available, verify its captured digest first. Never publish stale-source
      locations; clear obsolete findings through the existing cancellation and
      freshness lifecycle.

      Compilation exports current Design structure and runs native compile against
      stored projections. External producers own prepare and ingest; the editor
      neither reconstructs missing projections nor interprets eqval tables.
    }

    ArtifactVersionOwnership {
      package.json owns the extension artifact version.

      The extension Sigil contract does not duplicate its manifest-owned version
      literal.
    }

    ExtensionPackage {
      The extension bundles its client and Node-compatible server artifacts.

      Users require neither a globally installed Deno runtime nor a Sigil CLI.

      Distribution uses GitHub-hosted VSIX assets rather than Visual Studio
      Marketplace or Open VSX.
    }

    EditorLanguageSupport {
      The extension uses stable public VS Code APIs and vscode-languageclient.

      The extension bundles LspPackage and uses NavigationAndInspection for language
      intelligence without importing or duplicating core behavior.

      Document preview uses DocumentRendering through a standard server
      executeCommand and introduces no custom protocol method.

      Ownership file watching follows OwnershipSourceWatching, is notification-only,
      and does not modify workspace files.
    }

    DocumentPreview {
      Preview renders the whole active file and is not cursor-dependent.

      The editor-title action is visible only for Sigil editors, and the Command
      Palette action remains available.
    }

    SupportedExtensionHosts {
      extensionKind is workspace.

      Extension activation requires a trusted workspace under the current manifest
      behavior.

      File-backed desktop and remote Node hosts are supported; web and virtual hosts
      are not.
    }

    ReadOnlyEditorSupport {
      The extension edits no authored source and collects no telemetry. Explicit
      compilation uses disposable temporary captures and native disposable worlds.
    }

    RemovedWorkflowSurface {
      Delete commands, menus, activation hooks, process helpers and workspace-state
      bookkeeping dedicated to beams, accepted worlds, generated-view publication,
      handoffs and receipts. Those backend Tags are intentionally removed; their
      frontend needs no replacement or compatibility command. Keep authored-language
      diagnostics, navigation, highlighting and ordinary document preview. Retained
      compilation/status feedback has a separate native sigilc integration
      requirement.
    }

    CompilationSurface {
      Keep bundled language support and ordinary preview independent of installed
      compiler executables. Compilation needs current sigil and sigilc tools; show
      actionable settings guidance for missing or incompatible tools. Validate the
      final native result shape and its exit/state pairing before displaying success.
      Design Coherent/Loose and Implementation Closed/Converged exit 0; Design
      Disjoint and Implementation Drift exit 1. Exit 3 with an unavailable report
      leaves Implementation unset; operational failures and cancellation supply no
      completed semantic verdict. Bound stdout, stderr and process lifetime. Use argv
      arrays without a shell and clean temporary capture files after the child exits.
      Delete the old JSONL lifecycle, profiles, diagnostic history, stage UI and
      related tests. Do not parse Sigil or interpret eqval tables in the extension.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    EditorLanguageSupport {
      Decision: Bundle the Node-compatible Sigil language server and use standard LSP
      features, including whole-file preview via a standard executeCommand, without
      bespoke protocol methods.

      Scope: Governs the VS Code client-server integration and component preview;
      excludes Sigil language interpretation.

      Trade-offs: Bundling increases package size and requires rebuilding the server
      with the extension.

      Consequences: Preview, navigation, diagnostics, and highlighting remain
      compatible with the shared LSP contract.

      Revisit when: A separately installed shared server becomes operationally
      preferable.
    }

    DocumentPreview {
      Decision: Render the whole active file server-side through a standard
      executeCommand and display it with VS Code's built-in Markdown preview, exposed
      as a Command Palette command and an editor-title action, rather than a
      cursor-based hover preview or a duplicate renderer.

      Scope: Governs the preview behavior and its surfaces; excludes the server-side
      render projection, which SigilLsp owns.

      Design issues addressed: Users expect a Markdown-style whole-file rendered
      preview toggle, not a cursor-dependent single-component popup, and the
      extension must not depend on the CLI or duplicate core semantics.

      Consequences: The button and command render the whole file independent of
      cursor position; the previous cursor-based Show Component Preview command is
      removed; preview reuses the built-in Markdown preview and the server render
      capability.

      Revisit when: A custom rendered preview surface offers clearly better review
      ergonomics than the built-in Markdown preview.
    }

    OwnershipCacheInvalidation {
      Decision: Let the bundled language server dynamically register supported
      ownership-source watcher patterns through standard Language Server Protocol
      capability registration.

      Scope: Governs VS Code delivery of registered ownership-source changes;
      excludes ownership parsing, cache storage, and source-support policy.

      Trade-offs: Server-directed registration removes the redundant extension-owned
      watcher while depending on the language client's dynamic-registration support.

      Consequences: The extension does not duplicate supported source patterns and
      the server receives standard workspace/didChangeWatchedFiles notifications.

      Revisit when: The language client cannot reliably honor dynamic registration or
      the protocol provides incremental ownership-index updates.
    }

    SupportedExtensionHosts {
      Decision: Limit the initial extension to trusted file-backed desktop and remote
      Node workspace hosts.

      Scope: Governs the pre-production extension-host support contract; excludes
      future browser and virtual-workspace adapters.

      Trade-offs: Restricted, virtual, and browser workspaces receive no full
      extension support.

      Consequences: The manifest explicitly declares workspace placement and
      unsupported trust or virtual-workspace modes.

      Revisit when: The server supports browser or VS Code filesystem abstractions,
      or a security review approves a useful Restricted Mode subset.
    }

    ExternalCompilerIntegration {
      Decision: Use language CLI structural export and direct native sigilc final
      reports. The editor consumes native states and findings, not old events.
      Consequences: Both executables are configured independently; ordinary language
      support still needs neither. Remove compiler package dependencies.
    }

    LocationBasedCompilation {
      Decision: Name the focused action Compile File and use native ordered roots.
      Scope: Physical-file projections and native dependency closure. Design issues
      addressed: The new compiler scopes physical sources, so a cursor-based
      component label would misrepresent the compiled boundary. Consequences: File
      focus may include other components and dependency files; the native result
      exposes that membership. No duplicate language resolver or legacy
      component-selection adapter is introduced.
    }

    ExplicitAgenticCompilation {
      Decision: Keep compilation explicit and deterministic, without model calls.
      Consequences: Live language diagnostics remain independent; independent
      semantic reconstruction is external and missing prerequisites stay visible.
    }

    UserSelectedCompilationFocus {
      Decision: Let users select Design or Implementation from commands and the
      status item. Require explicit Implementation source selection and display
      native named states so yellow Converged is never mislabeled Drift.
    }
  }

  cases {
    EditorLanguageSupport {
      Opening a .sigil file applies TextMate highlighting and starts language
      features.

      Malformed source displays ranged diagnostics.

      Explicit Tag selections and resolved Tags support navigation, hover, and
      semantic highlighting.

      Changing an ownership annotation invalidates the server cache and the next
      component or Tag hover shows refreshed links.

      A failed bundled server produces visible output and an error without crashing
      VS Code.

      Deactivation stops the client and server.
    }

    DocumentPreview {
      Running preview on a Sigil file opens its whole-file Markdown render beside the
      editor.

      Selecting the editor-title action renders the same whole-file preview
      regardless of cursor position.

      Running preview when the language server is unavailable leaves editors
      unchanged and shows an informational message.
    }

    SupportedExtensionHosts {
      A trusted desktop or remote file-backed workspace receives full language
      support.

      Restricted Mode disables the extension under current manifest defaults.

      A virtual or web workspace receives no supported extension-host contract.
    }

    ExtensionPackage {
      A user installs the VSIX downloaded from GitHub Releases through Install from
      VSIX.
    }

    CompilationSurface {
      File compilation passes the workspace-relative active Sigil path as native
      scope root, independent of cursor position. Multi-component-file and invalid
      duplicate-name cases use the same physical-file boundary, with native ownership
      closure. Scope output exposes included dependency files and explicit root
      order. All six state/exit pairings display their native name and correct color;
      unavailable comparison remains unset. Reject malformed or mismatched results.
      The extension-host test exercises current structural export and native
      diagnostic projection, with separate fixed-report fixtures for gate states.
      Language navigation and ordinary Markdown preview still work. Removed workflow
      commands and the obsolete Compile Component alias are absent. Dirty documents
      prevent capture; edits and settings changes invalidate the displayed result.
      Failed/cancelled/superseded runs never overwrite a newer result. No profile or
      event-protocol arguments reach sigilc.
    }
  }
}
